{
  "policy_id": "ref-103-evict-foreign-nationals-from-social-housing",
  "party": "Reform UK",
  "policy_title": "Evict foreign nationals from social housing (three-month rule)",
  "measure": "D1",
  "verdict": {
    "measure": "D1",
    "shift": "toward more controlled",
    "net_migration": "lowers",
    "magnitude": "moderate",
    "confidence": "moderate",
    "plain": "This policy would require non-UK citizens in social housing to move to private rented accommodation within three months, and those who refuse could be deported \u2014 moving rules toward tighter control and potentially lowering net migration. The main uncertainty is how many people it would actually affect, since the group it targets is relatively small.",
    "biggest_unknown": "How many non-UK nationals currently in social housing would face deportation rather than comply or qualify for exemptions, and whether the policy would survive legal challenge, determines its actual effect on net migration.",
    "claims": [
      {
        "tier": "stated",
        "claim": "Non-UK citizens who fail to leave social housing within three months would lose their right to remain and face deportation.",
        "evidence_id": "M",
        "quote": "those who fail to do so would lose their right to remain in the UK and face deportation"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "In 2021, 7% of people living in social housing in England and Wales held a non-UK passport, indicating the directly affected group is relatively small.",
        "evidence_id": "E2",
        "quote": "7% of people living in social housing in England and Wales held a non-UK passport"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Current eligibility for social housing already requires settled status, ILR, or similar, meaning recent arrivals are largely excluded.",
        "evidence_id": "E6",
        "quote": "Eligibility typically requires naturalised citizenship, indefinite leave to remain (ILR), settled status under the EU Settlement Scheme, or specific humanitarian statuses like refugee status"
      },
      {
        "tier": "stated",
        "claim": "The policy targets foreign nationals currently legally eligible and resident in social housing.",
        "evidence_id": "E7",
        "quote": "Reform UK's policy appears to target even those foreign nationals who are currently legally eligible and resident in social housing"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "Reform UK also pledges to abolish Indefinite Leave to Remain, which would further restrict migrant eligibility for social housing and residence.",
        "evidence_id": "E8",
        "quote": "The party has also pledged to abolish Indefinite Leave to Remain, which would further restrict migrant eligibility"
      }
    ],
    "rationale": "The policy creates a direct deportation pathway for non-UK nationals who do not vacate social housing within three months, explicitly tightening enforcement and expanding grounds for removal. This moves policy toward more controlled immigration. The affected population is relatively small \u2014 around 7% of social housing residents hold non-UK passports \u2014 which limits the scale of net migration reduction, but the deportation mechanism and the linked pledge to abolish ILR represent a meaningful directional shift toward stricter control. Confidence is moderate because the size of the group ultimately deported (versus those who comply, qualify for exemptions, or successfully challenge removal) is unknown.",
